## Script Standards (this repo)

All plans you propose and all reusable code you write must follow these conventions. A plan that drafts code with violations is itself a violation. Covers: setup, check/audit, installer, deploy, hooks, shell aliases, and pseudocode in plans/*.md.

### Block order (setup scripts)

**Bash:** shebang/header > `set -euo pipefail` > `source aitools-lib.sh` + `logging_init "name"` (provides platform detection, display_path, read_config_key, log/log_ok/log_error/log_warn, write_warn/write_action/write_error, error_reason, write_summary, backup_file, backup_dir, emit_merge_details, ERRORS/WARNINGS counters) > OS guard > body > exit footer
**PS1:** header > `. aitools-lib.ps1` + `Initialize-Logging "name"` (provides ReadConfigKey, Log/LogOk/LogError/LogWarn, Write-AitWarn/Write-AitAction/Write-AitError, Get-AitErrorReason, Write-Summary, Backup-File, Backup-Dir, ConvertPSObjectToHashtable, Emit-MergeDetails, $errors/$warnings counters) > OS guard > body > exit footer

### Block order (check/audit scripts)

**Bash:** shebang/header > `set -euo pipefail` > `source check-lib.sh` + `source init-logging.sh` > OS guard (`log_error`) > `resolve_config` + `check_log_init "NAME"` > body > `print_summary`
**PS1:** header > `. check-lib.ps1` + `. init-logging.ps1` > OS guard (`LogError`) > `ResolveConfig` + `CheckLogInit "NAME"` > body > `PrintSummary`

### Error and warning tracking (required)

- Every warning goes through `log_warn`/`write_warn`/`write_action` (`LogWarn`/`Write-AitWarn`/`Write-AitAction`), every error through `log_error`/`write_error` (`LogError`/`Write-AitError`): one call logs, counts and records it once (`reference/logging.md` 8.2). Never raise `ERRORS`/`WARNINGS` (`$script:errors`/`$script:warnings`) or write a WARN or ERROR record by hand.
- Each script counts its own warnings; its error count also includes every relayed child that failed. A relayed child's warnings are not the parent's (`reference/logging.md` 8.3).
- Missing tracking is a bug -- an uncounted error misreports the exit status; an unrecorded warning or error is missing from the panel.

### Log line format

Every log line: `[timestamp] [script] [level] message`. Valid levels: `info`, `ok`, `warn`, `error`, `detail`. Console: red for `[error]`, yellow for `[warn]`, plain for `[info]`/`[ok]`. Log file: plain text only (no ANSI codes).

### Logging helpers

When to use each helper; its effects (deploy.log, counts, run summary record) are specified in `reference/logging.md` 8.2.

| Bash | PS1 | Level | When to use |
|------|-----|-------|-------------|
| `log` | `Log` | `info` | General info; a step the user explicitly asked for that completed as asked (`reference/logging.md` 8.3 "What is a warning") |
| `log_ok` | `LogOk` | `ok` | Success |
| `log_detail` | `LogDetail` | `detail` | Diagnostic content (file-only, no console) |
| `write_warn TOOL TEXT` | `Write-AitWarn` | `warn` | Warning about a known tool; TEXT says what is wrong |
| `write_action TOOL TEXT ACTION` | `Write-AitAction` | `warn` | Warning the user must act on; TEXT says what needs to be done, ACTION is the command |
| `log_warn TEXT` | `LogWarn` | `warn` | Warning when no tool is known (recorded under the script name) |
| `write_error TOOL WHAT REASON` | `Write-AitError` | `error` | Error: WHAT failed, REASON why |
| `log_error TEXT` | `LogError` | `error` | Only an error whose TEXT already states its cause (records an empty reason) |
| `error_reason OUTPUT EXIT_CODE` | `Get-AitErrorReason -Output -ExitCode [-ErrorRecord]` | `detail` (the output) | REASON for a failed external command, from its captured output |
| `write_summary OK\|DETAIL\|ACTION` | `Write-Summary` | none | Tool status, outcome items, an ACTION with no warning of its own |

A warning reports something wrong, or that still needs the user; anything else is info. UTC timestamps with Z suffix. Raw `echo`/`Write-Host` without structured logging is not acceptable. All helpers are defined in `scripts/aitools-lib.sh` / `.ps1` -- do not define inline copies. Apart from the writers in `reference/logging.md` 8.1, no script defines or overrides a logging function or appends to a log or run summary file directly; a script that needs other behaviour sets a library option (jsonl option, stderr console option: `reference/logging.md` sections 3, 5, 7).

### Agentic invocation logging

Every `invoke_ai` / `Invoke-AI` call must log structured telemetry to deploy.log:

| Event | Level | Format |
|-------|-------|--------|
| Invocation start | `info` | `AI invocation: speed=TIER backend=CLI attempt=N` |
| Success | `info` | `AI invocation: speed=TIER backend=CLI attempt=N result=accepted` |
| Validation failure | `warn` | `AI invocation: speed=TIER backend=CLI attempt=N result=rejected reason=REASON` |
| Rejected output | `detail` | `ai-rejected: LINE` (per-line, file-only) |
| All retries exhausted | `warn` | `AI invocation: speed=TIER backend=CLI exhausted after N attempts` |
| Backend unavailable | `error` | `AI invocation: no CLI available (claude or agent)` |

See `.claude/rules/agentic-standards.md` for prompt design and evaluation rules.

